                <p>We aren't affiliated with any of the groups below, and listing them here isn't an endorsement. Their programs, dates and costs change, so check with each one directly. If we've missed one, or something here is out of date, please <a href="/contact.html">let us know</a>.</p>
                <p>Looking for someone to repair your watch? The directory lists businesses, including some repairers; for individual watchmakers, start with the AWCI.</p>

                <h2>Organizations</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.awci.com/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Watchmakers-Clockmakers Institute (AWCI)</a>, Harrison, Ohio. The professional association for watchmakers and clockmakers in the US, founded in 1934. It runs certification exams, classes and webinars, and accredits schools through its Research and Education Council.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.nawcc.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Association of Watch and Clock Collectors (NAWCC)</a>, Columbia, Pennsylvania. A nonprofit for collectors and anyone interested in horology, with local chapters, a research library, the National Watch and Clock Museum and the School of Horology.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://hs-ny.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horological Society of New York (HSNY)</a>, New York. A nonprofit founded in 1866 and an affiliate chapter of AWCI. It offers watchmaking classes, monthly lectures, and scholarships and grants for watchmaking students and independent watchmakers.</li>
                </ul>

                <h2>Watchmaking schools and classes</h2>
                <p>The <a href="https://hs-ny.org/watchmaking-schools-programs" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Horological Society of New York's list</a> covers full-time watchmaking schools and part-time programs across the US, including those run by colleges, nonprofits and watch companies. <a href="https://www.awci.com/educationcareers/research-and-education-council-rec-schools-u-s/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWCI</a> lists the schools it accredits. Two schools that still appear on some lists, the Lititz Watch Technicum and the school at Oklahoma State University Institute of Technology, have closed.</p>
                <p>For shorter classes, see <a href="https://www.awci.com/educationcareers/awci-course-catalog/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">AWCI</a>, the <a href="https://www.nawcc.org/education/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">NAWCC School of Horology</a> and <a href="https://hs-ny.org/education" target="_blank" rel="noopener">HSNY</a>.</p>

                <h2>Museums</h2>
                <ul>
                    <li><a href="https://www.nawcc.org/museum/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">National Watch and Clock Museum</a>, Columbia, Pennsylvania. Run by the NAWCC.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://clockandwatchmuseum.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">American Clock &amp; Watch Museum</a>, Bristol, Connecticut. A museum devoted to American clocks and watches, founded in 1952.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://elginhistory.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Elgin History Museum</a>, Elgin, Illinois. Its largest exhibit covers the Elgin National Watch Company.</li>
                    <li><a href="https://www.charlesrivermuseum.org/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Charles River Museum of Industry &amp; Innovation</a>, Waltham, Massachusetts. On the industrial history of “Watch City,” including the Waltham Watch Company.</li>
                </ul>

